// @run
// @log 107
// @log 7
// @log 207
// @log s
// @log 307
// @log 2
// @info `a` is never reassigned; use `const` instead of `let`
// @info `a` is never reassigned; use `const` instead of `let`
// @warning Unused variable `g1` (prefix with `_` to suppress, or remove it)
// CONTRACT (must-RUN): D804's merged row reaches a `??` whose left operand is a CALL, and a
// `??` whose left operand has no second LITERAL at all.
//
// D842 closed the same defect where the left operand was a plain binding holding a literal;
// here the value arrives from further out, and the rungs are:
//   · the READER (`anonLeafJoinCallee`) marks the literals a CALLEE RETURNS as members of the
//     `??`'s join, so D804's leaf merge has two disagreeing atoms to mint a row from. It is
//     REFUTING as a whole: one return path it cannot name declines the entire callee, because
//     the merge also moves that callee's return row and a value left behind at the narrow row
//     would be handed back through a merged result.
//   · the RETURN ROW (`anonLeafSupersedeOf` at `fnRetStructIndexSid`, both functype arms and
//     `emitReturnValue`) carries the callee's signature and its returned literal to the merged
//     row together. Four sites and not one because each resolves the row separately; with any
//     of them missing the caller and the callee disagree at the seam.
//   · the ANNOTATION ATOM (`anonLeafAnnAtomOf`) is the other half of a family that has only
//     one literal: `let a: { r: i32 } | null = null` never writes `i32` anywhere, so without
//     the operand's own declared type the merge has one member and declines. It answers for an
//     IDENTIFIER operand only — see D872's row for the `loud -> silent` a CALL arm bought.
//
// THE READER'S REFUSAL IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT POLITENESS. `srcv` below returns a BINDING, which
// the reader cannot name; it keeps the narrow row and prints through it beside a merged family
// over the same field set. Redirecting every function whose return annotation resolves to a
// superseded row took that program from `runs` to check-clean invalid wasm — the one
// `runs`-losing movement this work found, and `anonLeafRetMoved` is the gate that answers it.
//
// MODULE SCOPE takes the same path (three cells in `distilled/named/d872_module_*`, nine in
// `d861_call_globalbind_*`) and is NOT a block here, for D842's reason: D864 makes a
// module-scope `??` over a nullable-struct global read out of bounds in `emitGlobalSection`
// once the compiler INSTANCE has compiled a layout twin, which is what this suite does.
//
// AND NEITHER IS A COMPETING SAME-FIELDSET DECLARATION, which is D873's own fixture
// (`coalesce-default-row-two-competing-rows.vl`). Two declared rows beside BOTH families in
// this file — the call-carried one and the annotation-only one — is a FOURTH ingredient that
// is check-clean invalid wasm on master and here alike: D874, found by this fixture and
// pinned whole in `distilled/named/d874_*`.

// ---- the callee's RETURN carries the family's other atom ----
function src(): { r: i32 } | null { { r: 7 } }

function rd() {
  const g1 = src() ?? { r: "s" }
  const v = g1.r
  if v is i32 { print(100 + v) } else { print(0) }
}
rd() // 107

// ---- the `null` corner: master's loud `bare null needs a struct-typed context` ----
function rn() {
  const g1 = src() ?? { r: null }
  if g1.r != null { print(7) } else { print(0) }
}
rn() // 7

// ---- and through a BINDING of the call, whose row comes from the callee's return ----
function rb() {
  const a = src()
  const g1 = a ?? { r: "s" }
  const v = g1.r
  if v is i32 { print(200 + v) } else { print(0) }
}
rb() // 207

// ---- NO SECOND LITERAL: the other atom lives only in the annotation ----
function ra() {
  let a: { r: i32 } | null = null
  const g1 = a ?? { r: "s" }
  print(g1.r)
}
ra() // s

// ---- the reader's REFUSAL: an unreadable callee keeps its narrow row beside a family ----
function srcv(): { r: i32 } | null {
  const v = { r: 7 }
  v
}

function uv() {
  const b = srcv()
  if b != null { print(300 + b.r) }
}
uv() // 307

function fam() {
  let a: { r: i32 } | null = { r: 1 }
  const g1 = a ?? { r: "s" }
  print(2)
}
fam() // 2
